<Base title="Installation count and privacy — DST" description="What DST's optional installation count reports, how to turn it off, and how connection IP addresses are handled.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Privacy" title="Installation count and privacy" intro="What is shared, what is stored, and how to stop reporting." />
  <section class="site-width" style="max-width:52rem;padding-block:2rem 5rem;line-height:1.8">
    <p>The Community page counts participating DST installations by edition. Only builds with installation reporting contribute. In current DST Docker builds, sharing is enabled by default. To turn it off, open <strong>Settings</strong> and disable the checkbox currently labelled <strong>Share anonymous usage count</strong>.</p>
    <p>Despite that label in current builds, reporting is not fully anonymous. A persistent random installation ID lets the service recognise repeat reports, and the receiving website and hosting provider can see the connection's source IP address.</p>
    <h2>What is reported</h2>
    <p>While DST is running, it sends a randomly generated installation ID, the Docker or Windows edition, and the DST app version to duneservertool.com every six hours. A generated reporting credential authenticates updates and removal of that installation's record. Server names, server addresses, Funcom credentials, account identities, players, configurations, logs, hardware IDs, and game data are not included.</p>
    <h2>What the website shows</h2>
    <p>The Community page shows two counts: DST for Windows / Solo, and DST Docker, for installations seen in the 24 hours before the displayed update time. The published snapshot updates automatically every six hours. There is no manual refresh control on the public website. The site operator can refresh the snapshot through authenticated SSH access. An installation is counted once even if it sends many reports. These are installations, not individual people, downloads, website visitors, maps, or public server listings. Hosts who opt out and older DST versions without this reporting feature are excluded.</p>
    <h2>Turning sharing off</h2>
    <p>Turning it off stops new presence reports and requests removal of the previous record. A successful removal deletes the count record; the public totals reflect that removal at the next snapshot update. If the count service is unavailable, removal is retried. New snapshots exclude reports older than 24 hours. The generated local identity is retained so an opt-in can reuse it. Updates preserve the setting and identity.</p>
    <h2>Connection information</h2>
    <p>The count database does not store IP addresses, request headers, server names, player details, or account identities. It stores hashes of the installation ID and reporting credential, the edition, app version, and the time of the latest report. The public page exposes only edition totals.</p>
    <p>The website's hosting access logs record connection IP addresses separately from the count database. Disabling cPanel's archive option stops keeping archived copies in the hosting account; it does not establish that the hosting provider has stopped recording requests. We do not promise that connection IP addresses are never logged, or that opting out removes provider logs. The 24-hour count expiry applies to count records, not hosting logs.</p>
    <h2>Availability</h2>
    <p>Reporting is a small background operation. A failed request does not block DST, server startup, game updates, or backups. Counts can be temporarily unavailable; the website does not replace an unavailable result with a made-up number.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
